    "body": "My take is continuing streaming after a fatal mode has thrown an error is a very bad idea, regardless of the encoding, and should be just forbidden for all encodings.\n\nDemo on utf-16:\n```js\nconst x = Uint8Array.of(1, 0xd8, 42, 0, 42, 0, 43, 0, 47, 0)\nfor (const fatal of [false, true]) {\n  console.log({ fatal })\n  const d = new TextDecoder('utf-16le', { fatal })\n  const chunk = (arr, stream) => {\n    try {\n      console.log(escape(d.decode(arr, { stream })))\n    } catch {\n      console.log('ERR')\n    }\n  }\n\n  chunk(x.subarray(0, 7), true)\n  chunk(x.subarray(7, 9), true) // have we reset state before this call?\n  chunk(x.subarray(9))\n}\n```\n\nResults in Chrome and Firefox/WebKit/Deno/Servo are different. And I would argue and both actually make no sense\n\nChrome does not reset the alignment of bytes, everything else does\n\nBut that reset makes decoding results differ from non-fatal mode on the same data\nResetting doesn't return a chunk of the data for some input chunks and errors for some chunks, it returns _different data_ for the chunks that did not error\n\nSame will happen for multi-byte encodings, as they contain state\n\n---\n\nEven on single-byte encodings, splitting fatal stream into different chunks _looses different data_\nDecoding should not be dependent on the shape of the chunks, and fatal streaming inherently breaks that",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
